::ALG1_U03_L04_Q01::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U03_L04_Q01<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Unit 3 graphing and slope support<br><br>Question:<br>In y = 4x + 9, what is the slope? {
=A. 4#Correct. In y = mx + b, m is the slope.
~B. 9#This is the y-intercept.
~C. 13#Adding the slope and intercept does not identify slope.
~D. (0, 9)#This is the y-intercept point, not the slope.




}

::ALG1_U03_L04_Q02::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U03_L04_Q02<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Unit 3 graphing and slope support<br><br>Question:<br>In y = -2x + 7, what is the y-intercept? {
~A. -2#This is the slope.
~B. (7, 0)#This reverses the intercept coordinates.
=C. (0, 7)#Correct. The y-intercept occurs when x = 0.
~D. 5#This adds -2 and 7.




}

::ALG1_U03_L04_Q03::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U03_L04_Q03<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Unit 3 graphing and slope support<br><br>Question:<br>Which equation has slope 3 and y-intercept (0, -5)? {
~A. y = -5x + 3#This reverses slope and intercept.
=B. y = 3x - 5#Correct. m = 3 and b = -5.
~C. y = 3x + 5#The intercept sign is wrong.
~D. y = -3x - 5#The slope sign is wrong.




}

::ALG1_U03_L04_Q04::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U03_L04_Q04<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Unit 3 graphing and slope support<br><br>Question:<br>Which graphing step is correct for y = 2x + 1? {
~A. Start at (2, 1).#The y-intercept has x = 0.
~B. Start at (1, 2).#This reverses slope and intercept.
~C. Start at (0, 2).#This uses the slope as the intercept.
=D. Start at (0, 1), then move up 2 and right 1.#Correct. b = 1 and m = 2.




}

::ALG1_U03_L04_Q05::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U03_L04_Q05<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Unit 3 graphing and slope support<br><br>Question:<br>A line has slope -3 and y-intercept (0, 4). What is its equation? {
=A. y = -3x + 4#Correct. Use y = mx + b.
~B. y = 4x - 3#This reverses slope and intercept.
~C. y = 3x + 4#The slope sign is wrong.
~D. y = -4x + 3#This uses the wrong slope and intercept.




}

::ALG1_U03_L04_Q06::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U03_L04_Q06<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Unit 3 graphing and slope support<br><br>Question:<br>A taxi charges $5 to start plus $2 per mile. Which equation models total cost y after x miles? {
~A. y = 5x + 2#This reverses starting fee and per-mile rate.
=B. y = 2x + 5#Correct. The rate is 2 per mile and the starting fee is 5.
~C. y = 7x#This treats the sum as a rate.
~D. y = x + 7#This uses a rate of 1.




}

::ALG1_U03_L04_Q07::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U03_L04_Q07<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Unit 3 graphing and slope support<br><br>Question:<br>In y = 1/2x - 6, what is b? {
~A. 1/2#This is the slope.
~B. 6#The sign matters; the constant is -6.
~C. (1, 2)#This is not the y-intercept.
=D. -6#Correct. b is the constant term.




}

::ALG1_U03_L04_Q08::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U03_L04_Q08<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Unit 3 graphing and slope support<br><br>Question:<br>Which equation has y-intercept (0, 0)? {
~A. y = x + 4#The y-intercept is (0, 4).
~B. y = 4x - 1#The y-intercept is (0, -1).
~C. y = -2x + 3#The y-intercept is (0, 3).
=D. y = 5x#Correct. This has b = 0.




}

::ALG1_U03_L04_Q09::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U03_L04_Q09<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Unit 3 graphing and slope support<br><br>Question:<br>What does m represent in y = mx + b? {
=A. The slope or rate of change#Correct. m tells how y changes for each 1-unit change in x.
~B. The y-intercept#The y-intercept is b.
~C. The x-intercept#Slope-intercept form does not label m as the x-intercept.
~D. The output when x = 0#That describes b, the y-intercept, not m.




}

::ALG1_U03_L04_Q10::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U03_L04_Q10<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Unit 3 graphing and slope support<br><br>Question:<br>What does b represent in y = mx + b? {
~A. The rate of change#The rate of change is m; b is the starting output when x = 0.
~B. The run#Run is part of slope, not b.
~C. The x-value#b is an output value when x = 0.
=D. The y-intercept#Correct. b gives the output when x = 0.




}

::ALG1_U03_L04_Q11::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U03_L04_Q11<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Unit 3 graphing and slope support<br><br>Question:<br>Which point is the y-intercept of y = -4x + 8? {
~A. (-4, 0)#This uses the slope as an x-coordinate.
=B. (0, 8)#Correct. The y-intercept is where x = 0.
~C. (8, 0)#This reverses the y-intercept coordinates.
~D. (0, -4)#This uses the slope as the y-value.




}

::ALG1_U03_L04_Q12::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U03_L04_Q12<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Unit 3 graphing and slope support<br><br>Question:<br>Which equation has slope 0 and y-intercept (0, 3)? {
~A. y = 3x#This has slope 3 and intercept 0.
~B. y = x + 3#This has slope 1, so it does not match the required slope of 0.
=C. y = 3#Correct. A horizontal line has slope 0 and y-intercept 3.
~D. y = -3x#This has slope -3 and intercept 0.




}

::ALG1_U03_L04_Q13::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U03_L04_Q13<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Unit 3 graphing and slope support<br><br>Question:<br>Graph y = -x + 2. Which second point can be found from the y-intercept using the slope? {
=A. (1, 1)#Correct. Start at (0, 2), then move down 1 and right 1.
~B. (1, 3)#This uses a positive slope.
~C. (2, 0)#This is also on the line but uses two slope steps, not one second point from one step.
~D. (0, -1)#This changes the intercept incorrectly.




}

::ALG1_U03_L04_Q14::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U03_L04_Q14<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Unit 3 graphing and slope support<br><br>Question:<br>A line starts at 10 and increases by 4 each input step. Which equation matches? {
~A. y = 10x + 4#This reverses rate and starting value.
~B. y = 14x#This treats the sum as a rate.
=C. y = 4x + 10#Correct. The slope is 4 and the y-intercept is 10.
~D. y = x + 14#This uses a rate of 1.




}

::ALG1_U03_L04_Q15::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U03_L04_Q15<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Unit 3 graphing and slope support<br><br>Question:<br>Which equation matches slope -5 and y-intercept (0, 2)? {
~A. y = 5x + 2#The slope sign is wrong.
~B. y = 2x - 5#This reverses slope and intercept.
~C. y = -2x + 5#This uses the wrong values.
=D. y = -5x + 2#Correct. m = -5 and b = 2.




}

::ALG1_U03_L04_Q16::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U03_L04_Q16<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Unit 3 graphing and slope support<br><br>Question:<br>What is the slope of y = x - 7? {
~A. -7#This is the y-intercept.
=B. 1#Correct. x means 1x, so the slope is 1.
~C. 7#This ignores the coefficient of x.
~D. 0#The equation has an x-term.




}

::ALG1_U03_L04_Q17::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U03_L04_Q17<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Unit 3 graphing and slope support<br><br>Question:<br>What is the y-intercept of y = -6x? {
=A. (0, 0)#Correct. There is no constant term, so b = 0.
~B. (0, -6)#This uses the slope as the intercept.
~C. (-6, 0)#This uses the slope as an x-coordinate.
~D. (6, 0)#This is not the y-intercept.




}

::ALG1_U03_L04_Q18::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U03_L04_Q18<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Unit 3 graphing and slope support<br><br>Question:<br>Which interpretation fits y = 12x + 20 for a membership plan? {
~A. $12 starting fee and $20 per month#This reverses slope and intercept.
=B. $20 starting fee and $12 per month#Correct. b = 20 and m = 12.
~C. $32 per month with no starting fee#This adds the two values incorrectly.
~D. $12 total after 20 months#This does not match the equation structure.




}

::ALG1_U03_L04_Q19::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U03_L04_Q19<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Unit 3 graphing and slope support<br><br>Question:<br>Which equation matches a graph crossing the y-axis at (0, -4) with slope 2? {
~A. y = -4x + 2#This reverses slope and intercept.
~B. y = 4x + 2#This uses the wrong intercept and sign.
=C. y = 2x - 4#Correct. m = 2 and b = -4.
~D. y = -2x - 4#The slope sign is wrong.




}

::ALG1_U03_L04_Q20::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U03_L04_Q20<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Unit 3 graphing and slope support<br><br>Question:<br>Which equation has a negative slope and positive y-intercept? {
~A. y = 3x + 2#The slope is positive.
~B. y = -3x - 2#The y-intercept is negative.
=C. y = -3x + 2#Correct. The slope is negative and the intercept is positive.
~D. y = 3x - 2#The slope is positive and intercept is negative.




}

::ALG1_U03_L04_Q21::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U03_L04_Q21<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Unit 3 graphing and slope support<br><br>Question:<br>A student says the y-intercept of y = 7x + 2 is 7. What is the misconception? {
=A. The student confused the slope with the y-intercept.#Correct. 7 is slope, and 2 is the y-intercept value.
~B. The student forgot that x is the y-intercept.#x is the input variable, not the intercept.
~C. The student should add 7 and 2.#Adding does not identify equation features.
~D. The student found the x-intercept correctly.#The statement is about y-intercept, not x-intercept.




}

::ALG1_U03_L04_Q22::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U03_L04_Q22<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Unit 3 graphing and slope support<br><br>Question:<br>Which graphing move matches slope 3/2? {
~A. Down 3 and right 2#That matches slope -3/2.
~B. Up 2 and right 3#That matches slope 2/3.
=C. Up 3 and right 2#Correct. Rise 3 over run 2 gives slope 3/2.
~D. Right 3 and down 2#That matches slope -2/3.




}

::ALG1_U03_L04_Q23::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U03_L04_Q23<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Unit 3 graphing and slope support<br><br>Question:<br>Which graphing move matches slope -1/4? {
~A. Up 1 and right 4#That matches positive 1/4.
=B. Down 1 and right 4#Correct. Rise -1 and run 4 gives slope -1/4.
~C. Down 4 and right 1#That matches slope -4, not -1/4, because the run is only 1.
~D. Up 4 and right 1#That matches positive slope 4, not negative slope -1/4.




}

::ALG1_U03_L04_Q24::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U03_L04_Q24<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Unit 3 graphing and slope support<br><br>Question:<br>Which equation matches a line with y-intercept 6 and slope -2? {
~A. y = 6x - 2#This reverses slope and intercept.
~B. y = 2x + 6#The slope sign is wrong.
~C. y = -6x + 2#This uses the wrong slope and intercept.
=D. y = -2x + 6#Correct. m = -2 and b = 6.




}

::ALG1_U03_L04_Q25::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U03_L04_Q25<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Unit 3 graphing and slope support<br><br>Question:<br>Why is slope-intercept form useful for graphing? {
=A. It gives the y-intercept and slope directly.#Correct. These two features help locate and draw the line.
~B. It always gives the x-intercept directly.#The form directly gives the y-intercept, not always the x-intercept.
~C. It removes the need to plot points.#You still use points to draw the line.
~D. It only works for horizontal lines.#Slope-intercept form works for many non-vertical linear equations.




}
